The vectors are drawn on a square grid, and $\mathbf{c}=\overrightarrow{OC}$.

![Vector diagram](vector-675629dc3ad3640d79d27a19-prompt.png)

Given that $\mathbf{c}=\lambda\mathbf{a}+\mu\mathbf{b}$, find $\lambda$ and $\mu$.

$\lambda=$ {{field-1}}

$\mu=$ {{field-2}}

question.:question/worked-solution:
![Completed vector route](vector-675629dc3ad3640d79d27a19-solution.png)

Construct the route $O\to X\to C$, first parallel to $\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\mathbf{a}$.

Along $OX$, move $6$ squares left and $6$ squares up. A copy of $\mathbf{b}$ moves $4$ squares right and $4$ squares down. Thus $OX=\frac{3}{2}|\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{OX}=-\frac{3}{2}\mathbf{b}$.

Along $XC$, move $6$ squares up. A copy of $\mathbf{a}$ moves $2$ squares down. Thus $XC=3|\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{XC}=-3\mathbf{a}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{c}&=\overrightarrow{OX}+\overrightarrow{XC}\\&=-\frac{3}{2}\mathbf{b}-3\mathbf{a}\\&=-3\mathbf{a}-\frac{3}{2}\mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}
$$

Reorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\lambda=-3$ and $\mu=-\frac{3}{2}$.

